hey've ramped up  their higher education system so these  the human capital is just exploded in  China and that's what's driven their  growth recognizing that you need a lot  of transportation to move the goods and  services around for that kind of growth  they've built a lot of infrastructure so  you know if the government is going to  be responsible for infrastructure you  wouldn't want a short shrift that and  hold back your economic  grow the bad side is of course that this  is a highly authoritarian government  highly authoritarian society they've not  held back from displacing millions of  people in order to build this  infrastructure there's no property  rights there so they've done what they  want when they want and how they want  which when it works you know looks like  an economic benefit because you're not  counting the cost all the people who are  displaced or pushed aside and when it  doesn't work or when you have problems  of corruption which still you know crop  up not infrequently in China there's a  lot of resources they get siphoned off  or wasted I don't actually find rush  hour in Chinese cities to be that  radically different from rush hour in  Chicago or Los Angeles you know they're  wide freeways in there you know  wall-to-wall cars going you know half a  car length at a time very you know what  I experienced on the 405 in LA is a lot  of what I expect on the third Ring Road  in Beijing whenever I go there so stop  and go congestion is the same no matter  what the scenery is like in a sense in  China the the big difference is you see  a lot of other people traveling by means  other than cars still what you don't see  in the US I mean in the u.s. outside of  New York in cities like Chicago in San  Francisco you still got more than 80% of  the people traveling by car so yeah you  might see the train go by in Chicago or  San Francisco or the BART line or  whatever but you don't see people a lot  of people walking you don't see a lot of  people biking there is every city in  China that you travel through by car  when you're stuck in traffic you see  that there's a lot of people walking  there's a lot of people biking there's a  lot of people on those buses and trains  they're full they're not like the empty  buses and trains  you see in most US cities so that's the  real difference is there  they really do use all the modes of  transportation much more so than we do  in the u.s. because in the u.s. we're  wealthier and everybody has cars you  know that I've always been fascinated by  the fact that if you're a poor person in  a city in the United States and you do  not own a car you borrow more rides from  friends with cars then you ride the  transit system according  to National Statistics so you know it's  an interesting system where bumming a  ride is a more better system for most  people than using the public transit  system in China it's nothing like that  it's a much more so many people don't  own cars cars are so new there they  travel by other means and they just take  it as a matter of course transportation  planners in the US you know think we a  lot more of us should be walking and  biking and so forth is there always  making this argument that we should get  out of our cars and I say you know  everywhere I go in the world where  that's actually occurring all those  people are trying to get into cars and  that's not just China that's Europe  that's Asia that's everywhere now is the  Chinese cities are getting congested as  the number of cars in these cities is  exceeding the capacity of the road  system that they built even with all the  new roads they have built in all the new  roads they are building the government's  wrestling with how do we deal with this  congestion and they're not willing to  trade off their growth in that industry  in order to reduce congestion so  experimenting with congestion pricing  they're looking at how do they build  better systems to manage their  transportation networks using a lot of  new technology adopting new technologies  much faster than we do in the US and you  know they're more willing to keep  building as they grow you know in the  u.s. we've almost stopped building urban  transportation infrastructure or at  least automobile infrastructure even  though the number of automobiles and the  distance people are driving and the  number of people driving them keep going  up so we're cramming more and more into  the same size bag they're cramming more  and more to the same size bag but  they're recognizing they got to get a  bigger bag as they're building out their  interstate highway system which they're  in the early stages of building that  national interstate highway network they  are planning to make an awful lot of it  toll roads because that's the only way  they can combine I mean they need that  sort of revenue to build the things in  the first place and in the second place  if they don't have tolls on them they'll  just get congested so they they want to  use pricing you know scientifically in a  sense to figure out you know if the  price gets too high we have to build  more Road in the u.s. we much more have  the attitude at least at the local level  transportations a problem and if people  moved less we'd have less of a problem  and so we're not trying to figure out  how do we make our transportation system  better in our cities so we can be more  economically competitive and China  they're constantly thinking about how do  we make the transportation system in our  town work better so we can be more  economically competitive the idea that  infrastructure is important to our  economy and that infrastructure  investments can have a positive effect  on getting out of recession makes sense  the question is where does that come  from and what drives it having the  central government try to pump more you  know money distributed through political  systems is not the right way to do it if  we you know borrow a lot of money from  the future to pay for building a lot of  infrastructure now that will help us get  out of the recession which i think is  the wrong headed way stimulus hasn't  worked it doesn't work  borrowing just means that when we do get  out of the recession there'll be less  money then to spend then because we've  already borrowed it all so it just it  flattens out our climb out of the  recession for the foreseeable future  it's a terrible way to approach things  having local projects that can be done  you know executed by folks who are  willing to pay for it and most  importantly I think recognizing there's  a lot of private money willing to invest  in roads where people are willing to pay  to use those roads and I should say  somewhat the same at a lesser extent for  transit systems if there's there are bus  lines and projects out there on public  transit where enough people are willing  to pay for it where you might be able to  get private capital to invest to build  that thing but it's overwhelmingly true  for a whole number of projects on the  road side and we're still stuck in this  sort of you know we're here we have this  very capitalist culture and country and  we have this incredibly socialist  transportation system where the idea of  having the private sector build and run  a road is just crazy talk and over in  China where it's an a valid Lee  communist system the idea of having the  private sector build and run a road is  like well yeah how else are we going to  build all the roads yeah so how crazy is  it that we're that backwards in the u.s.  that we can  we can just barely wrap our brains  around the concept of the private sector  building a doggone road so we've got to  overcome that we've got to embrace that  to build the transportation system we  need to get everybody where they want to  go especially think 20 years we better  have a lot more transportation by then  or we're all just going to be staying  home  [Music]

